WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.901 --> 00:00:02.234
-That's got to feel good.

00:00:02.234 --> 00:00:03.901
A little standing ovation
for you, buddy. Welcome back.

00:00:03.901 --> 00:00:05.801
You know this is your 10th
appearance on our show?

00:00:05.801 --> 00:00:09.734
-Yeah, I was joking, you know,
trying to pick a shirt to wear.

00:00:09.734 --> 00:00:11.133
I had on a shirt and I was like,

00:00:11.133 --> 00:00:12.968
"I think I've worn this
on the show."

00:00:12.968 --> 00:00:16.033
Five times,
I had an emergency change.

00:00:16.033 --> 00:00:18.300
-Yeah, well, you look good
in whatever you wear, bud.

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:20.167
I want to congratulate you
on the book.

00:00:20.167 --> 00:00:21.501
Look at this right here,
come on.

00:00:21.501 --> 00:00:22.934
"The Ravaged."

00:00:22.934 --> 00:00:26.000
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:28.434
You know what? I was going to
ask you, because we've been --

00:00:28.434 --> 00:00:29.767
I've asked you every
single question,

00:00:29.767 --> 00:00:32.567
but you've been in
a bunch of music videos

00:00:32.567 --> 00:00:34.234
with some major people.

00:00:34.234 --> 00:00:39.267
I mean like Keith Richards,
Lady Gaga -- I love that one.

00:00:39.267 --> 00:00:42.300
Bjork.
-Bjork, Radiohead, Orville Peck.

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:43.734
Yeah, a whole bunch of them.

00:00:43.734 --> 00:00:45.934
-Do they come to you
or do you find them?

00:00:45.934 --> 00:00:48.968
How do you get in all
these cool music videos?

00:00:48.968 --> 00:00:53.200
-You know, when I first --
I lived in downtown L.A.,

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:54.667
where nobody lived down there,

00:00:54.667 --> 00:00:57.567
and they kind of all new each
other -- all these directors.

00:00:57.567 --> 00:00:59.000
And they kind of passed me
around.

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:01.234
So I did a bunch of them, but --

00:01:01.234 --> 00:01:02.567
-What was Keith Richards like?

00:01:02.567 --> 00:01:04.868
-Keith was cool.
He kind of singled me out.

00:01:04.868 --> 00:01:06.801
And he goes, "Come with me,"
to his trailer.

00:01:06.801 --> 00:01:09.167
And I was like, "Oh, my God.
It's Keith Richards."

00:01:09.167 --> 00:01:11.367
And I'm sort of sitting on
the sofa like that,

00:01:11.367 --> 00:01:12.667
and somebody is doing his hair.

00:01:12.667 --> 00:01:14.501
And he goes, "Norman, you know
how I got this haircut?"

00:01:14.501 --> 00:01:17.234
And I go, "No."
And he goes, "My mom gave me

00:01:17.234 --> 00:01:18.567
money as a kid to get a haircut,

00:01:18.567 --> 00:01:20.067
and I kept the money
and cut it myself.

00:01:20.067 --> 00:01:22.567
And it's been like this
ever since."

00:01:22.567 --> 00:01:23.934
And I was like, "I love you."

00:01:23.934 --> 00:01:26.133
-That's a rad Keith Richards
story.

00:01:26.133 --> 00:01:28.067
I want to talk about
"The Walking Dead."

00:01:28.067 --> 00:01:30.234
I know you just finished filming
the final episode.

00:01:30.234 --> 00:01:31.534
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:31.534 --> 00:01:34.367
They're going to air this fall.

00:01:34.367 --> 00:01:36.734
How was it the last day on set?

00:01:36.734 --> 00:01:38.367
Was it emotional?
-Yeah.

00:01:38.367 --> 00:01:39.834
There was a lot of drinking.

00:01:39.834 --> 00:01:41.467
There was confetti cannons.

00:01:41.467 --> 00:01:43.834
-Confetti cannons are good,
yeah.

00:01:43.834 --> 00:01:47.000
-They hand me the mike.

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:49.033
I don't know.
It sort of felt surreal.

00:01:49.033 --> 00:01:52.300
And then a friend of my mine,
Jess, she taught me a phrase.

00:01:52.300 --> 00:01:55.100
"Is the juice worth
the squeeze?"

00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:56.901
And I love that phrase.

00:01:56.901 --> 00:01:59.434
And I kind of said it
to the group.

00:01:59.434 --> 00:02:01.067
I was like, you know,
"'The Walking Dead' juice

00:02:01.067 --> 00:02:02.267
has always been
worth the squeeze."

00:02:02.267 --> 00:02:04.133
Then I realized how perverted
that sounds.

00:02:04.133 --> 00:02:05.367
[ Laughter ]

00:02:05.367 --> 00:02:07.467
-No, but I understand.
-You know what I mean?

00:02:07.467 --> 00:02:09.467
-Yeah, "Is the juice worth
the squeeze?"

00:02:09.467 --> 00:02:11.267
That's a lot of work to do
these shows.

00:02:11.267 --> 00:02:12.534
-Oh, my God, it's a ton.

00:02:12.534 --> 00:02:14.501
-And you've been on for was it,
11 seasons now?

00:02:14.501 --> 00:02:17.000
-11 seasons, 12 years
we've been on.

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:19.033
I mean, wait.
Yeah, 12 years, 11 seasons.

00:02:19.033 --> 00:02:21.167
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:21.167 --> 00:02:26.167
Yeah, you know, this last
season, too, was a full year,

00:02:26.167 --> 00:02:28.133
which was exhausting.
-Yeah.

00:02:28.133 --> 00:02:29.501
-And you're in the woods
and you're crying

00:02:29.501 --> 00:02:31.901
and you're killing and you're
screaming and fighting.

00:02:31.901 --> 00:02:34.367
You know, you've seen the show.
-Exactly.

00:02:34.367 --> 00:02:36.300
-It's not "Succession" where

00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:38.767
you're, like, on a yacht
having caviar.

00:02:38.767 --> 00:02:40.000
[ Laughter ]

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:41.734
We're eating squirrels
and killing each other.

00:02:41.734 --> 00:02:44.767
-Yeah, that would be
a little disappointing.

00:02:44.767 --> 00:02:46.467
-"Succession," call me,
by the way.

00:02:46.467 --> 00:02:47.934
[ Laughter ]

00:02:47.934 --> 00:02:51.167
-This is you right here on your
first episode when Daryl --

00:02:51.167 --> 00:02:52.667
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:52.667 --> 00:02:54.501
You survived.
-Yeah.

00:02:54.501 --> 00:02:55.667
-It's also been announced that

00:02:55.667 --> 00:02:57.534
you're going to have
a spin-off series.

00:02:57.534 --> 00:02:59.701
Daryl is having
a spin-off series.

00:02:59.701 --> 00:03:02.234
Do you know anything about that?
Do you have any details?

00:03:02.234 --> 00:03:04.601
-Yeah, I know it's going to be
much different than the show.

00:03:04.601 --> 00:03:07.367
You know, it was originally
Carol and I --

00:03:07.367 --> 00:03:08.834
Melissa McBride and I.
-Yeah.

00:03:08.834 --> 00:03:10.667
-And she's taking time off.

00:03:10.667 --> 00:03:14.501
Like, you know, 12 years is
a grueling schedule,

00:03:14.501 --> 00:03:15.634
and she wanted to take
some time off.

00:03:15.634 --> 00:03:18.367
So she's doing that
and she deserves it.

00:03:18.367 --> 00:03:21.767
And they kind of came to me --
I imagine those characters

00:03:21.767 --> 00:03:23.133
are going to meet back up
at some point.

00:03:23.133 --> 00:03:25.167
And I might even meet up with
some other characters

00:03:25.167 --> 00:03:27.467
down the road.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:27.467 --> 00:03:30.667
-Alright. So good.
-But she's taking time off.

00:03:30.667 --> 00:03:32.000
In the meantime, they're like,

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:33.501
"Hey, do you want to go
on a mission?"

00:03:33.501 --> 00:03:35.801
And I'm like, "Yeah."
-"Yeah, let's do it."

00:03:35.801 --> 00:03:37.167
Speaking about going
on a mission,

00:03:37.167 --> 00:03:38.968
congrats on this novel, bud.
-Aww, thanks.

00:03:38.968 --> 00:03:40.567
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:40.567 --> 00:03:44.000
-This is a real book.

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:45.467
I mean, this is a real novel.

00:03:45.467 --> 00:03:48.033
This is like --
I put out kids books.

00:03:48.033 --> 00:03:49.367
[ Laughter ]

00:03:49.367 --> 00:03:52.434
This is my book.
It's like that.

00:03:52.434 --> 00:03:53.634
That's my books there.

00:03:53.634 --> 00:03:55.367
And then this is -- Look.

00:03:55.367 --> 00:03:57.834
It's almost the same thickness
really if you think about it.

00:03:57.834 --> 00:03:59.667
Yeah, that's yours.

00:03:59.667 --> 00:04:01.634
-I'm stealing yours
on the way out.

00:04:01.634 --> 00:04:02.968
-[ Laughs ]

00:04:02.968 --> 00:04:04.734
What made you want to
write a book?

00:04:04.734 --> 00:04:06.834
-You know, I've been approached
a couple times

00:04:06.834 --> 00:04:09.267
about writing books
about my life.

00:04:09.267 --> 00:04:12.868
But I don't want to get beat up,
so I'm not going to do that.

00:04:12.868 --> 00:04:14.434
Maybe later on down the road.

00:04:14.434 --> 00:04:16.601
-Yeah. I can tell you have
too many stories.

00:04:16.601 --> 00:04:18.901
-Yeah, I don't want people
knocking on my door.

00:04:18.901 --> 00:04:22.334
But, you know, I have
a production company,

00:04:22.334 --> 00:04:24.567
so I'm always looking for
stories

00:04:24.567 --> 00:04:26.534
to make into TV shows
or whatever.

00:04:26.534 --> 00:04:29.000
And I took a bunch of
different stories.

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:33.434
And they all kind of have
the same theme of finding family

00:04:33.434 --> 00:04:35.968
along -- you know,
along the road, in,

00:04:35.968 --> 00:04:37.467
like, the weirdest places.

00:04:37.467 --> 00:04:40.567
Kind of like "Walking Dead" I
guess in a way, in hindsight.

00:04:40.567 --> 00:04:44.334
But there's a friend of mine
who used to hop trains.

00:04:44.334 --> 00:04:46.968
I kind of took his story,
and I kind of created that.

00:04:46.968 --> 00:04:49.234
I was shooting "Ride,"
and I was on an airplane

00:04:49.234 --> 00:04:52.601
coming from Uruguay
to Costa Rica.

00:04:52.601 --> 00:04:55.234
-I love "Ride."
-Yeah, it's fun.

00:04:55.234 --> 00:04:56.400
And we're about to start
another one.

00:04:56.400 --> 00:04:58.400
Keanu and I are doing Utah.

00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:00.467
-No way.
-Yeah, next week.

00:05:00.467 --> 00:05:04.267
But anyway, I'm sitting next to
this businessman on a plane,

00:05:04.267 --> 00:05:06.400
and I'm covered in dirt
and I smell really bad.

00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:07.701
And I have a backpack
at my feet

00:05:07.701 --> 00:05:09.067
and sand is coming out of it.

00:05:09.067 --> 00:05:11.501
And people are coming by going,
"Can I get a picture,

00:05:11.501 --> 00:05:13.300
an autograph," or whatever.

00:05:13.300 --> 00:05:16.767
He goes, "Who are you?"
And I said, "I'm on a TV show.

00:05:16.767 --> 00:05:18.033
That's what that's about."

00:05:18.033 --> 00:05:20.701
And he starts talking to me,

00:05:20.701 --> 00:05:23.434
and he runs one of the largest
Fortune 500 companies

00:05:23.434 --> 00:05:26.000
in the world.
Don't ask which one.

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:28.934
But he had a series of
events happen,

00:05:28.934 --> 00:05:31.734
and he found himself kind of
out in the world traveling.

00:05:31.734 --> 00:05:33.033
So I go,
"You should go to Chile."

00:05:33.033 --> 00:05:34.334
He'd be like, "Yeah, okay."

00:05:34.334 --> 00:05:36.968
He was bouncing around trying to
find himself kind of.

00:05:36.968 --> 00:05:39.100
And he's looking at me with
tears in his eyes,

00:05:39.100 --> 00:05:42.434
and he's like, "I've spent
my whole life making money.

00:05:42.434 --> 00:05:44.534
I've wasted my whole life."

00:05:44.534 --> 00:05:46.434
And it just hit me,

00:05:46.434 --> 00:05:49.801
and I sort of ran with a story
sort of based on that.

00:05:49.801 --> 00:05:51.234
A whole bunch of
different things.

00:05:51.234 --> 00:05:52.634
-That's the best.
Take those stories

00:05:52.634 --> 00:05:54.834
and make them into a nice novel.

00:05:54.834 --> 00:05:57.200
Congratulations on this.
It's a great book.

00:05:57.200 --> 00:06:00.534
Norman Reedus, everybody.
Check out "The Ravaged,"

00:06:00.534 --> 00:06:02.634
which is available on May 10th.

